# vitepress cache directory
**/.vitepress/cache

# Docusaurus cache and generated files
.docusaurus

# Serverless directories
.serverless/

# FuseBox cache
.fusebox/

# DynamoDB Local files
.dynamodb/

# TernJS port file
.tern-port

# Stores VSCode versions used for testing VSCode extensions
.vscode-test

# yarn v2
.yarn/cache
.yarn/unplugged
.yarn/build-state.yml
.yarn/install-state.gz
.pnp.*

# Local Netlify folder (legacy host; site now deploys to Firebase Hosting)
.netlify
.netlifyignore
.claude/worktrees/

# Deploy artifacts — generated by build/deploy scripts, never commit
deploy-*.zip
*.zip

# Vite HMR transients. Vite creates these while hot-reloading the config
# and occasionally forgets to clean them up. They'd pile up in git
# (~210 currently committed) if we don't explicitly ignore them.
vite.config.*.timestamp-*.*

# Local build-test folders. We've accumulated 18+ of these (~7 MB each) —
# they're just `npm run build` outputs under a different name. Generate,
# don't commit.
dist_test*/
dist-test*/

# Playwright browser profiles created by tests. Tens of MB of binary
# Chromium cache — has no business being in version control. It is also a
# CREDENTIAL-SHAPED tree: a Chromium profile carries Network/Cookies,
# Login Data, Trust Tokens, passkey_enclave_state, and a Local State holding
# os_crypt key material. This directory was tracked from April 2026 until it
# was untracked, and that key material is what raised secret-scanning alert #1
# (the stores themselves were empty in every committed version — see
# docs/security/AUDIT_LOG.md).
.playwright/

# Per-session scratch from the Playwright CLI: dated `page-<timestamp>.yml`
# snapshots of whatever page the run was looking at, written into the
# directory the tool was started from. Debris, not a harness — and a snapshot
# of an authenticated page is exactly the sort of thing you do not want
# accruing in a public repo.
.playwright-cli/

# Per-session scratch from the Playwright MCP server (.mcp.json): page
# snapshots, traces and downloads, written into whatever directory the
# server was started from. Session debris, not source.
.playwright-mcp/

# Skills written into the working tree by a tool's own installer — e.g.
# `playwright-cli install --skills`, which generates .claude/skills/playwright-cli/.
# Vendoring generated tooling means re-syncing it by hand every time the
# installer changes; anyone who wants these runs the installer. Hand-authored
# Claude tooling (.claude/agents/, .claude/launch.json) stays tracked, and so
# do deliberately vendored third-party skills, each negated by name below.
.claude/skills/*
!.claude/skills/ian-xiaohei-illustrations/

# Firebase Functions build output.
functions/lib/

# Firebase CLI deploy cache (hosting.*.cache) — regenerated by every
# `firebase deploy`/`vite build` cycle and meaningless to anyone but the
# CLI; versioning it just churned unrelated diffs.
.firebase/

# Firebase / Google service config files contain API keys — never commit.
# Each environment generates its own from the Firebase console.
google-services.json
**/google-services.json
GoogleService-Info.plist
**/GoogleService-Info.plist

# Android release signing — NEVER commit. Losing the keystore means
# losing the ability to update the app on Play Store. See
# docs/ANDROID-RELEASE.md for where to store securely.
*.keystore
*.jks

# Server-side credentials — NEVER commit. Firebase Admin SDK / GCP
# service-account keys grant full backend access; private keys (SSH/TLS/PEM)
# are equally fatal if leaked. Backend secrets live as Firebase Functions
# secrets, never as files in the tree. (Enforced by scripts/test-secret-hygiene.mjs.)
*serviceAccount*.json
*service-account*.json
*-firebase-adminsdk-*.json
*.pem
id_rsa
id_dsa
*.ppk

# Generated demo trial credentials — never commit
scripts/demo-credentials.csv
scripts/demo-credentials*.csv
scripts/demo-names.local.txt
scripts/demo-names.*.local.txt
.tmp/

# Locally generated images (e.g. scripts/kie-generate-image.mjs output).
generated/

# Migration / export artifacts (e.g. output/<tool>-migration-<timestamp>/) can
# contain live WhatsApp (Baileys) session material — creds.json plus the
# *-key-*.json / app-state-sync-*.json files — which must NEVER be committed.
# Quarantine the migration export trees and any credentials/session folder no
# matter where a tool drops it.
output/*-migration-*/

# Scratch trees + one-off report dumps (Phase 6 repo hygiene, 2026-08-15).
# `tmp/` and `output/` are session scratch space — debug uploads, ad-hoc
# browser snapshots — and are never consumed by code or CI. The QA/report
# dumps are write-only run outputs (nothing reads them back as a baseline),
# so they live untracked where a run drops them. The narrower migration
# quarantine above stays on its own line so that credential concern survives
# even if these blanket ignores are ever narrowed.
tmp/
output/
.auth-qa-report.json
.authoring-qa-report.json
.payments-check.json
# Stray zero-byte artifact a shell redirect once left at the repo root.
/firebase
**/credentials/
creds.json
**/creds.json
app-state-sync-*.json
sender-key-*.json
pre-key-*.json
session-*.json

# 2013 curriculum migration artifacts (generated by scripts/migrate-2013-curriculum.mjs;
# the app normalizes the raw curriculum-data-2013.json at read time, so these are
# audit/report outputs only and are not consumed at runtime).
public/syllabi/migration-2013-report.json
public/syllabi/curriculum-2013-normalized.json
functions/data/curriculum-2013-normalized.json
public/syllabi/_backups/

# Visual regression run output (§4.6). Rendered pages, diffs and summaries from
# `npm run visual:gate` — published as CI artefacts, never committed. The
# BASELINES under tests/visual/baselines/ are committed, and only ever by a
# reviewed workflow — baseline-bootstrap.yml records ones that do not exist,
# baseline-update.yml replaces one that does. Never by hand: a baseline carries
# the environment that drew it, so a locally recorded one is un-comparable in CI.
tests/visual/output/
preview-shots/

# One-off account-cleanup backups (scripts/cleanup-resurrected-profiles.mjs).
# These hold the PROFILE DATA of users who asked to be deleted — emails
# included — so committing one would be a worse and more permanent disclosure
# than the resurrection bug they exist to clean up. Written mode 0600; delete
# them once the cleanup is confirmed.
resurrected-profiles-backup-*.json
